<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunac Ceel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hunac Ceel is a figure of Maya tradition recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/books-of-chilam-balam">Books of Chilam Balam</a> as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> rule at Mayapan in northern Yucatán. According to the chronicles he survived being thrown into the sacred cenote at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, proclaimed a prophecy and later brought down the lord of Chichen Itza. The accounts are partly legendary and their dating is uncertain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunac-ceel.jpg" alt="Hunac Ceel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salhedine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cenote_Xtoloc_en_Chich%C3%A9n_Itz%C3%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hunac Ceel, also called Cauich and known in full as Hunac Ceel Cauich, is described as a Maya general from Telchaquillo. The Maya history attributes the war he led to the kidnapping of a powerful ruler's wife by a powerful lord. Other explanations have been offered: the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> may have been partly descended from Toltec outsiders, disliked as foreign oppressors, or the war may have been a simple one of conquest. Both the attackers and the attacked are, however, labelled Itzá in the sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the account in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/books-of-chilam-balam">Chilam Balam</a> of Chumayel, in the chapter called The Rise of Hunac Ceel, he fought the Itzá unsuccessfully and was taken captive. Ah Mex K'uuk threw him into the sacred cenote as a sacrifice to the gods, but he survived a whole night in the water and spoke a prophecy of the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaac</a> about the coming harvest. With Ah Mex K'uuk as his sponsor he became lord of Mayapan, which with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> and Uxmal ruled the north of Yucatán. With the help of highland forces he then attacked Chichen Itza, overthrew its ruling elite and left Mayapan sole ruler of the region.</p><p>In his commentary on the book, the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-roys">Ralph L. Roys</a> proposed that someone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> had carried off the bride of Ah Ulil, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izamal">Izamal</a>, and that Hunac Ceel and his Mexican allies used this as a pretext or reason to sack the city in Izamal's name and to reorder politics throughout the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His actions upset the balance of power in northern Yucatán and changed the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peten-itza">Lake Petén Itzá</a> area, where the Itzá were still living at the time of European contact. The feature film Kings of the Sun opens with his forces attacking Chichen Itza, with Leo Gordon in the part, and a Mexican folk dance and music group named Hunac-Ceel was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hunac Ceel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The books of Chilam Balam, Maya chronicles written down in colonial times, tell how <b>Hunac Ceel</b>, head chief of Mayapan, drove Chac Xib Chac, ruler of Chichen Itza, from his city with the help of captains bearing Nahuatl names, an episode they place in a Katun 8 Ahau,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial sources such as the Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel say <b>Hunac Ceel</b> of Mayapan conquered the city in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/work/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mayapan, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Mayapan, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.63,-89.46&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Mayapan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2141 1400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hunac Ceel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2591 1670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2591 1670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.63,-89.46&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
